  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does a receptor do?",
      "a": "A receptor is a cell, or group of cells, that detects a change, called a stimulus, such as heat, pressure or light. It turns it into an electrical nerve impulse.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the receptor in the skin"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens in the spinal cord during a reflex?",
      "a": "The sensory neurone passes the impulse to a relay neurone in the spinal cord, which passes it straight to a motor neurone. The brain is not needed to start the response.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the spinal cord"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is an effector?",
      "a": "An effector is the part that responds, usually a muscle that contracts or a gland that releases a substance. In this reflex it is the arm muscle.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the arm muscle"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do I feel pain after I have already moved?",
      "a": "The reflex goes through the spinal cord, which is a short route. The message to the brain, where pain is felt, arrives a moment later.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the brain",
        "Why do I pull away before it hurts?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are reflexes useful?",
      "a": "Reflexes are fast and automatic, so they protect you from harm, such as burning, falling or getting dust in your eye, without wasting time thinking.",
      "asks": [
        "What are other examples of reflexes?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A reflex arc runs: stimulus, receptor, sensory neurone, relay neurone, motor neurone, effector, response.",
    "The fastest nerve impulses in humans travel at over 100 metres per second."
  ],
